<h3 id="%F0%9F%92%A1-Key-Findings-&amp;-HR-Insights:">💡 Key Findings &amp; HR Insights:<a class="anchor-link" href="#%F0%9F%92%A1-Key-Findings-&amp;-HR-Insights:">¶</a></h3><ul>
<li><p>The Experience Gap: There is a clear experience gap between the two groups. Employees who stay with the company have a higher average of total working experience (11.8 years), whereas those who leave average significantly less experience (8.2 years).</p>
</li>
<li><p>The 10-Year Retention Threshold: Attrition heavily spikes and concentrates among junior to mid-level professionals with less than 10 years of overall work experience, as indicated by the prominent blue stacks on the left side of the histogram.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Senior Executive Stability: Once an employee accumulates more than 12 to 15 years of total working experience, their likelihood of leaving drops to near-zero, proving that senior talent is highly retained and loyal to the organization.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Strategic HR Recommendation: The first decade of a career is the highest risk phase for turnover. HR must introduce targeted career acceleration plans, mid-career retention bonuses, and senior mentorship tracks specifically for employees in the 5-10 years experience bracket to protect the company's future leadership pipeline.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
