<h3 id="%F0%9F%92%A1-Key-Findings-&amp;-HR-Insights:">💡 Key Findings &amp; HR Insights:<a class="anchor-link" href="#%F0%9F%92%A1-Key-Findings-&amp;-HR-Insights:">¶</a></h3><ul>
<li><p>The Promotion Paradox: Interestingly, the data shows that employees who left the company actually had a lower average time since their last promotion (1.9 years) compared to those who stayed (2.2 years).</p>
</li>
<li><p>Hidden Discontent: This proves that a lack of recent promotion is <em>not</em> a direct driver for overall attrition in the company. Employees are resigning despite being promoted recently, which heavily implies other factors like burnout (Overtime) or uncompetitive baseline salaries are responsible.</p>
</li>
<li><p>HR Recommendation: Instead of assuming promotions solve retention issues, HR should investigate post-promotion workload, compensation adjustments, and role satisfaction, as talent is still draining shortly after upward mobility.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
